</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span>When target knowledge is forgotten, LLMs lose focus on <span id="A7.F12.6" class="ltx_text ltx_ulem_uline ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">KeyTokens</span> ; When target knowledge is retained, LLMs still focus on <span id="A7.F12.7" class="ltx_text ltx_ulem_uline ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">KeyTokens</span>. <span id="A7.F12.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BA6666;">Redder</span> token means more positive contribution; <span id="A7.F12.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#8080C6;">Bluer</span> token means more negative contribution. This figure is continued from <a href="#A7.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix G Why Unlearning Can Work? ‣ Understanding the Dilemma of Unlearning for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 11</span></a>.</figcaption>
